  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1216 AM CDT Thu Aug 6 2026\n\nFog... Patchy ground fog has already begun developing overnight\ndue to decoupling winds and clear skies, reducing visibilities to\n1 SM or less in some areas. CAMs continue to show a strong signal\nfor areas of locally dense fog persisting through late this \nmorning, before dispersing within a few hours of sunrise. Will\ncontinue to observe trends over the next few hours to determine\nwhether or not an advisory will be needed. \n\nRain/storm chances... Will continue to message potential for several\nrounds of rain/storms late this week through early next week, \nthough the synoptic pattern remains volatile. However, details \ncontinue to come into focus for storm timing and severity on \nFriday as upper perturbation interacts with cold FROPA at the \nsurface during the afternoon and evening. Corridor of decent\ninstability (1,000 to 1,200 J/kg) builds just ahead of the front \nduring this time, with potential for storms to start off surface-\nbased. Gusty winds look to be the main hazard with any strong or \nsevere storms as bulk shear exceeds 30 knots and inverted-V \nprofiles tap into 35 to 40 knot winds at the top of the dry layer.\nSevere threat decreases further south and east.\n\nBeyond Friday, general story hasn't changed much as we remain on \nthe northern periphery of western ridge and more moist/unstable \nairmass. As such, better chances for more organized convection\nlook to remain off to our south where better thermodynamics \nreside. ML guidance continues to highlight southern Wisconsin,\nnorthern Illinois, and even into the Ohio Valley for the greatest\nsevere threat. In our neck of the woods, multiple embedded \nshortwaves riding the 500 mb wave train should be enough to kick \noff several rounds of showers and non-severe storms this weekend \nthrough early next week. Though predictability of timing and\ncoverage of showers/storms remains low this far out due to the \nnature of the synoptic pattern, question that arises will be\nexcessive rainfall potential due to repeated rounds of precip.\nSimilar to the severe threat, better moisture looks to be holed up\nto our south in proximity to the low, though some long-range\nguidance suggests the boundary stalling out over southern\nWisconsin. Current thinking is that any heavy rain potential will\nhinge on training storms and mesoscale processes. Will continue \nto monitor in the coming days, as WPC begins highlighting a \nmarginal ERO for Day 5.\n\nTemperatures/smoke... Temperatures to read near normal in the low\nto mid 80s through the weekend as upper flow flattens out and \nweakens. Humidity levels will be comfortable, with dewpoints \nreading in the upper 50s to mid 60s. Another plume of elevated\nwildfire smokes works its way into Wisconsin behind the front on\nFriday, though significant impacts to visibility or air quality\nare expected.",
